    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## In infinite dimensions, equivalence fails\n\nOn $C([0, 1])$, the sup-norm $\\|f\\|_\\infty$ and the $L^2$-norm $\\|f\\|_2$ are not equivalent. For $f_n(x) = x^n$:\n\n- $\\|f_n\\|_\\infty = 1$ for every $n$.\n- $\\|f_n\\|_2^2 = \\int_0^1 x^{2n}\\,dx = 1/(2n+1) \\to 0$.\n\nSo $\\|f_n\\|_\\infty / \\|f_n\\|_2 \\to \\infty$ — no $C$ can bound the ratio. The two norms induce different topologies on the same vector space.\n\nThis is one big reason functional analysis is genuinely harder than $\\mathbb{R}^n$ analysis: you have to *choose* a norm, and your choice changes everything.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Verify numerically */\nfor n in [1, 2, 5, 10, 50, 100] do\n  print(\"n =\", n, \":  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 = \",\n        float(sqrt(integrate(x^(2*n), x, 0, 1))))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "n = 1 :  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 =  0.5773502691896258\nn = 2 :  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 =  0.4472135954999579\nn = 5 :  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 =  0.30151134457776363\nn = 10 :  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 =  0.2182178902359924\nn = 50 :  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 =  0.09950371902099892\nn = 100 :  ‖x^n‖_∞ = 1,  ‖x^n‖_2 =  0.07053456158585983"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Bounded vs continuous linear maps\n\nA linear map $T : X \\to Y$ between normed spaces is **bounded** if $\\|T x\\|_Y \\le M\\,\\|x\\|_X$ for some $M$ and all $x$. The smallest such $M$ is the **operator norm** $\\|T\\|$.\n\nTwo equivalences for linear maps:\n\n- **Bounded** ⇔ **continuous** ⇔ continuous at $0$.\n\nIn finite dimensions every linear map is bounded; in infinite dimensions, unbounded ones exist (e.g., differentiation $C^1([0,1]) \\to C([0,1])$ with sup-norm). We'll come back to this in notebook 3.\n\n## What we have\n\nThe infrastructure is in place:\n\n- Vector space + norm + completeness = Banach space.\n- $C([a,b])$, $L^p$, $\\ell^p$ — the canonical examples.\n- Finite vs infinite dimensions: equivalence vs not.\n- Linear maps split into bounded and unbounded.\n\nThe next notebook adds *inner product structure* — Hilbert spaces, where geometry returns.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}